## Size and position

LUI uses **logical units**: the host scales them for the display. For a dimension,
the resolved value is `parent size × Scale + Offset`. UDim2 holds one such pair
for width/X and another for height/Y.

```luau
Button.Size = UDim2.fromOffset(180, 44) -- A fixed logical size.
Panel.Size = UDim2.new(1, -32, 1, -32) -- Parent size minus 32 on each axis.
Panel.Position = UDim2.fromOffset(16, 16) -- A 16-unit top/left margin.
```

This fragment assumes Button and Panel already exist. For the panel above,
the remaining 16 units on the right/bottom complete the margin. Use
[UDim2](../../api/globals/udim2/) for a complete example.

`Position` identifies the point selected by AnchorPoint. With AnchorPoint (0,0),
that is the top-left corner. With (0.5,0.5), it is the center. AbsoluteSize and
AbsolutePosition are the resolved measurements; read them rather than assigning
them.

## Arrange a list

Replace Src/Main.luau in a basic starter with this complete example:

{{Example:UIListLayout}}

**Expected result:** three vertically stacked buttons with eight-unit gaps.
UIListLayout arranges their positions; LayoutOrder chooses their order. Use
UIPadding for space around the container and UIGridLayout for a grid.

## Scroll longer content

{{Example:ScrollingFrame}}

**Expected result:** a short viewport shows part of a taller list. CanvasSize
declares the scrollable extent; CanvasPosition requests an offset. The final
clamped value is AbsoluteCanvasPosition. ScrollingFrame clips its children.

## Handle actions before raw input

Use `Button.Activated` for an ordinary action. It represents supported button
activation rather than only a mouse press. For a custom right-click menu or
drag gesture, use InputBegan/InputChanged/InputEnded and check Device before
reading pointer-only fields. IsCanceled distinguishes a canceled pointer gesture
from a completed release. The [input example](../../api/types/inputevent/) and
[complete right-click recipe](../recipes/#right-click-input-and-a-custom-menu)
show the difference.

## Clean up a screen

```luau
local Screen = Instance.new("Frame", {Parent = Window})
local Button = Instance.new("TextButton", {Parent = Screen, Text = "Continue"})
Button.Activated:Connect(function() Button.Text = "Ready" end)

-- When leaving this screen:
Screen:Destroy()
```

This fragment assumes Window exists. Destroying Screen also destroys the button
and disconnects its signals. Calling Destroy again is safe. Setting Parent to
nil only detaches an object; it does not destroy it.

Keep shared application data outside the screen if it must survive navigation.
Service subscriptions and work started independently by the application need
their own cleanup. The [modular tutorial](../../learn/advanced-project/) shows a
screen switch that destroys the old screen before creating the next.

## Text input and deeper details

Use TextChanged to read committed Text. An input method can temporarily compose
text before committing it; that preview is separate. Custom selection uses
zero-based Unicode scalar offsets (code points), not UTF-8 byte offsets or
necessarily visible-character counts. Ordinary fields do not need custom
selection handling. Read [TextBox](../../api/classes/textbox/) when implementing
an editor or secret-entry flow.

Inspect Layout starts a separate copy of the app’s Luau state. It helps inspect
the shared tree and geometry, but it does not attach to your existing native
window or qualify host-specific input behavior.
